% ============================================================================
% cloud-type — each common cloud type and the weather it indicates
% (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% An ELEVENTH science slice, and a genuinely new axis from the already-shipped
% `meteorology/weather-instruments.adj` (instrument -> quantity it measures)
% and `oceanography/ocean-observing-instruments.adj` (instrument -> quantity)
% — this table does not name an instrument at all. It names a cloud TYPE and
% the weather condition its presence indicates:
% `cloud_type(cloud, weather_indication)`. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? cloud_type(cirrus, $W)   % approaching_warm_front
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `cloud_type(cloud, weather_indication)` carrying the NWS citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% weather indication WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a cloud not one of the three the cited page names (e.g.
% altocumulus). The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a weather indication and
% recall the cloud type that signals it:
%
%     ? cloud_type($C, heavy_rain_thunderstorm)   % cumulonimbus
%
% The three cloud types and the weather each one indicates, as a little
% truth table:
%
%     cloud          weather_indication            the cited quote
%     ------------   ---------------------------   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     cirrus         approaching_warm_front         "They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level
%                                                     jet streak."
%     cumulonimbus   heavy_rain_thunderstorm        "a mature, deep cumulonimbus cloud, i.e., a thunderstorm producing heavy rain."
%     stratus        light_rain_drizzle_or_none     "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover
%                                                     which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation
%                                                     or drizzle."
%     (the page has a NON-BREAKING SPACE, U+00A0, after "precipitation-free",
%      which this comment cannot show; the table shipped an ordinary space there
%      until 2026-09-15, a string the page never wrote. The row's `source` now
%      carries the page's character.)
%
% Provenance: the National Weather Service's (Louisville forecast office)
% "Cloud Classification" education page. EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN CLOUD'S
% SENTENCE AS ITS `source` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986); the envelope used to be
% the cirrus span, the primary source of the cumulonimbus and stratus answers
% too, and is now the page's framing sentence, "Clouds are classified according
% to their height above and appearance (texture) from the ground.", which
% names no cloud. This paragraph used to say the quotes were "verbatim" and
% "WebFetch-verified before writing"; a raw fetch on 2026-09-15 found the
% stratus quote written with an ordinary space where the page has U+00A0 (see
% the note in the truth table), so that claim is withdrawn rather than
% restated. Measured then: each row sentence and the envelope occur exactly
% once. (The related jetstream.noaa.gov domain had 403'd WebFetch, which is why
% weather.gov was used.) `trust authoritative` — weather.gov is a primary U.S.
% government (NWS) science-education source, the same tier
% `weather-instruments.adj`'s and `precipitation-types.adj`'s NOAA/NWS
% sources earned. A cloud not one of these three — altocumulus, nimbostratus,
% cumulus — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a
% weather indication. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table cloud_type {
    columns cloud, weather_indication

    row (cirrus, approaching_warm_front) {
        source "Cirrus clouds are wispy, feathery, and composed entirely of ice crystals. They often are the first sign of an approaching warm front or upper-level jet streak."
    }
    row (cumulonimbus, heavy_rain_thunderstorm) {
        source "If enough atmospheric instability, moisture, and lift are present, then strong updrafts can develop in the cumulus cloud leading to a mature, deep cumulonimbus cloud, i.e., a thunderstorm producing heavy rain."
    }
    row (stratus, light_rain_drizzle_or_none) {
        % U+00A0 after "precipitation-free", as on the page.
        source "Stratus clouds are uniform and flat, producing a gray layer of cloud cover which may be precipitation-free or may cause periods of light precipitation or drizzle."
    }

    % THE ENVELOPE WAS THE CIRRUS SPAN, and so the primary source of the cumulonimbus and stratus answers.
    % Each row now carries its own cloud's sentence; this framing
    % sentence names no cloud and no weather signal.
    source "Clouds are classified according to their height above and appearance (texture) from the ground."
    locator "https://www.weather.gov/lmk/cloud_classification"
    trust authoritative
}
